# the aarch64 selection table: rules, constants and the classes that hold values
#
# every instruction is a row of masc's catalog and every class a class of its
# register file, each named by the handle masc publishes for it. a rule is an
# ir pattern, a guard, an expansion and a cost. the extensions a row needs are
# masc's admission of it, so an lse, cssc or sve rule is passed over on a build
# without the extension and the rule after it in cost is chosen.
#
# an integer or pointer is held in an x register and a word computes in its w
# view, which the allocator names through the view of the x virtual. a narrow
# integer's bits above its width are unspecified, so every reader that needs
# them extends, and a condition is 0 or 1. a float of either width and a
# vector are held in a v register, a scalar row naming its s or d view. the
# flags are written and read within one expansion, never held.
#
# a cost is two for each instruction an expansion emits, with a multiply
# counting three, a divide twelve and a loop twelve. it is less by one for
# each instruction the pattern covers and by one when the rule takes its
# constant operand as an immediate, so a rule that covers more costs less
# than the rules for the pieces. a branch on a declassified condition, a
# division's trap guard, costs less than cbnz on any condition, so the guard
# is always a compare and a separate b.cond whose flags its declassify point
# names, never a compare and branch on the value.
#
# a symbol is reached through mink's neutral aarch64 relocation kinds, which
# every format maps, the hook of each address rule reading the format the
# target names where elf and mach-o reach it differently.

use std.types.bool.false;
use std.types.bool.true;

use rf: masc.core.register;
use masc.isa.aarch64.gen_register;
use masc.isa.aarch64.gen_row;
